-- Q5822: Data Protection Technologies [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-52 Rev. 2, TLS protects data only during transit between endpoints. When messages are decrypted at the server for compliance scanning, they exist in plaintext in server memory, creating a potential point of compromise distinct from end-to-end encryption architectures.
Wrong (A): TLS 1.3 cipher suites are cryptographically strong. The residual risk lies in the architectural decision to decrypt at the server, not in the transport protocol''s cipher strength.
Wrong (B): The compliance scanning process does not permanently weaken encryption. Messages are re-encrypted after scanning using the same cryptographic standards.
Wrong (D): Re-encryption does not inherently introduce integrity errors. TLS includes integrity verification mechanisms that detect any message corruption during processing.$EXPL$
