-- Q5827: Data Protection Technologies [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-175B, symmetric encryption algorithms such as AES use a single shared secret key for both encryption and decryption operations. This makes symmetric encryption fast and efficient for bulk data processing.
Wrong (A): Using two mathematically related keys describes asymmetric (public-key) encryption, not symmetric encryption. Symmetric systems use one shared secret key.
Wrong (B): Generating a fixed-length hash digest describes hashing, not encryption. Hash functions are one-way and do not support decryption.
Wrong (C): Relying on a certificate authority describes PKI-based identity verification, which supports asymmetric encryption but is not a defining characteristic of symmetric encryption.$EXPL$
