-- Q5838: Data Protection Technologies [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-63B, salting password hashes defeats precomputed attacks like rainbow tables. Each unique salt means the same password produces a different hash for each user, forcing an attacker to brute-force each account individually rather than using a single precomputed lookup table.
Wrong (A): Salts do not encrypt the hash output. A salt is concatenated with the password before hashing; it does not add a second layer of encryption.
Wrong (B): Salts do not increase the SHA-256 output length. SHA-256 always produces a 256-bit output regardless of input length or salt presence.
Wrong (D): Salts do not add time delays to hash operations. Rate-limiting is the function of adaptive hash functions like bcrypt or Argon2, not salts themselves.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5838;

